<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  App Transport Security is relaxed so the app can talk to a self-hosted
	  server over plain HTTP on the local network.

	  NSAllowsLocalNetworking alone is not enough, which is easy to get wrong:
	  Apple's local-networking exception covers only dotless hostnames, .local
	  domains, and *link-local* addresses (169.254.0.0/16, fe80::/10). A NAS at
	  an RFC 1918 address like 192.168.1.10 is not covered, and ATS blocks it.

	  NSExceptionDomains cannot fill the gap either: it takes domain names, not
	  IP literals, and the server's address is whatever the user types at
	  runtime — there is nothing to declare ahead of time.

	  That leaves NSAllowsArbitraryLoads. The trade-off is real: it disables
	  TLS enforcement for every connection the app makes, not just LAN ones.
	  It is acceptable here only because the app has exactly one network peer —
	  the user's own MediaVault server — and contacts nothing else. If that ever
	  stops being true, this needs revisiting.

	  NSAllowsLocalNetworking is kept alongside it: it is honoured for .local
	  hostnames, so pointing the app at `nas.local:8000` stays on the narrow
	  path rather than relying on the blanket exception.
	-->
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>

	<!--
	  Required since iOS 14 for any connection to a device on the local network.
	  Without this key the system denies access without even prompting.
	-->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>MediaVault connects to your MediaVault server on this network to browse your media library.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
